        "requirement": "Nothing else added beside or on top of it",
        "status": "met",
        "evidence": "Only the base and its drawers. No neighbouring cells, nothing stacked on top, and no end caps or joint bolts."
      },
      {
        "requirement": "Five drawers in it",
        "status": "unmet",
        "evidence": "Only three fit: drawer:0#0,0#cut, drawer:1#0,0#cut and drawer:2#0,0#cut, one per storey. A half base gives one drawer per storey, a quarter or full base gives none, and the configurator offered no further drawer spots."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "I couldn't fit five drawers in one hex base. The configurator caps it at three, so the build has a single base with three drawers.\n\n- **Full base:** a full hex base has no cut face, so it takes no drawers.\n- **Half base:** a half base takes one drawer, on its cut face.\n- **Quarter base:** a quarter base takes none.\n- **What I built:** I replaced the starting full base with one 3-storey north half base (fused, at q0 r0) and put a drawer on the cut face of each storey. That's three drawers. After that the configurator offered no more drawer spots.\n\ncheck_build passes with no rule errors and meshClash reads \"clear\". Nothing else is beside or on top of the base. I didn't add end caps or lock the joints (the usual finishing steps), because you asked for nothing else to be added. The base has 9 open faces that could take a cap if you want them closed. The part count is 13 because the configurator counts the drawers' parts separately.",
    "outcome": "compromise"
  },
